    "summary": "High‑energy summer show with stellar Reuben & Cherise and tight jams",
    "blurb": "The set opens with a breezy Picasso Moon and builds through a punchy Reuben And Cherise that shines throughout the night. The second set delivers Crazy Fingers into a seamless Playing In The Band → He's Gone stretch and a soaring The Other One. Overall the band is on fire, offering crisp guitar tones and solid rhythm.",
    "review": "June 9, 1991 at Buckeye Lake was a sun‑blasted, feel‑good summer outing that left the Deadheads buzzing. The first set rolled smoothly from Picasso Moon into a bright Reuben And Cherise that many reviewers called the night’s high point, while Jerry’s guitar sparkled with “great tone” and solid opening chords. The second set kicked off with Samson & Delilah before launching into a fiery Crazy Fingers that propelled the band into an immaculate Playing In The Band → He's Gone sequence, followed by a generous drum‑and‑space excursion that birthed a soaring The Other One and a rollicking Wharf Rat. The encore’s Knockin’ On Heaven’s Door capped a night where the group was “on fire” from start to finish.\n\nAcross the board, recordings capture the band in fine form: Charlie Miller’s soundboard tape is praised for its crystal‑clear mix, the matrix version adds the ambience of a hot summer day, and even audience tapes convey the palpable excitement despite occasional muffling. With tight interplay, bright guitar work, and an infectious vibe, this show stands out as a quintessential early‑’90s Dead experience.",
